    def shortestMatchingSubstring(self, s: str, p: str) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Pattern p splits into three parts: prefix A, middle B, suffix C (where p = A*B*C). A substring matches if it starts with A, ends with C, and contains B in between in order. For each occurrence of A at position i, find the first occurrence of B at j ≥ i+len(A), then the first occurrence of C at k ≥ j+len(B). The length is k + len(C) - i. Using str.find with sorted occurrence lists, I'll compute this efficiently with precomputed occurrence lists and bisect.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-7649", "code": "import bisect\n\nclass Solution:\n    def shortestMatchingSubstring(self, s: str, p: str) -> int:\n        first = p.index('*')\n        second = p.index('*', first + 1)\n        A = p[:first]\n        B = p[first + 1:second]\n        C = p[second + 1:]\n\n        def occurrences(pat):\n            res = []\n            if pat == \"\":\n                return list(range(len(s) + 1))\n            start = 0\n            while True:\n                idx = s.find(pat, start)\n                if idx == -1:\n                    break\n                res.append(idx)\n                start = idx + 1\n            return res\n\n        oa = occurrences(A)\n        ob = occurrences(B)\n        oc = occurrences(C)\n        if not oa or not ob or not oc:\n            return -1\n\n        best = -1\n        lb, lc = len(B), len(C)\n        for i in oa:\n            # first B occurrence starting at or after i + len(A)\n            t = bisect.bisect_left(ob, i + len(A))\n            if t == len(ob):\n                continue\n            j = ob[t]\n            # first C occurrence starting at or after j + len(B)\n            u = bisect.bisect_left(oc, j + lb)\n            if u == len(oc):\n                continue\n            k = oc[u]\n            length = k + lc - i\n            if best == -1 or length < best:\n                best = length\n        return best\n"}

