Starter code:

class Solution:
    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is a binary search on the answer d, with a feasibility check: can we pick k boundary points with pairwise Manhattan distance ≥ d?

Mapping boundary points to a 1D perimeter coordinate t in [0, 4·side) makes the check easier. Along the boundary, Manhattan distance between two boundary points is at least the perimeter distance between them only in some cases, so I'll use the standard approach: for a fixed d, greedily pick points in perimeter order, and for the cyclic selection, try each starting point, always taking the next point whose perimeter position is at least d past the last chosen one, and checking the wrap-around gap back to the start. The greedy-on-cycle with a fixed start is the standard technique for this problem, and checking all starts makes it exact for the cyclic structure.

Feasibility(d): sort points by perimeter coordinate; for each start index s, greedily select points with perimeter gap ≥ d from the previous chosen one, up to k points, and require the last chosen point to be at perimeter distance ≥ d from the start going around the cycle. If any start yields k points, d is feasible. Binary search for the largest feasible d.

I'll submit this.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-3554", "code": "from typing import List\nimport bisect\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\n        P = 4 * side\n\n        def to_t(x, y):\n            # map boundary point to perimeter coordinate in [0, 4*side)\n            if y == 0:\n                return x                  # bottom edge, left to right\n            if x == side:\n                return side + y           # right edge, bottom to top\n            if y == side:\n                return 3 * side - x       # top edge, right to left\n            return 4 * side - y           # left edge, top to bottom (x == 0)\n\n        ts = sorted(set(to_t(x, y) for x, y in points))\n        n = len(ts)\n\n        def feasible(d):\n            if d == 0:\n                return True\n            for s in range(n):\n                start = ts[s]\n                chosen = 1\n                last = start\n                i = s\n                while True:\n                    # next index with ts >= last + d\n                    target = last + d\n                    j = bisect.bisect_left(ts, target, i + 1)\n                    if j >= n:\n                        break\n                    last = ts[j]\n                    i = j\n                    chosen += 1\n                    if chosen == k:\n                        break\n                if chosen >= k:\n                    # wrap-around: last to start must also be >= d along the cycle\n                    if P - last + start >= d:\n                        return True\n                    # otherwise try trimming the last point; greedy already maximal\n            return False\n\n        lo, hi = 0, side\n        while lo < hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2\n            if feasible(mid):\n                lo = mid\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n        return lo\n"}

