class Solution:
    def longestSpecialPath(self, edges: List[List[int]], nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:


=== MODEL ===
I'll solve this with a DFS that maintains the current root-to-node path, tracking for each value the most recent depth it appeared at on the path. The window of allowed start positions is bounded by the last occurrence of any repeated value, so I keep a left boundary that moves forward.

Let the path from root to current node be nodes with prefix distances `dist[d]` (cumulative length) and count of nodes at each depth. When entering node v at depth D with value x, if x already occurred at depth `last[x]` on the current path (and that depth is ≥ the current left boundary), the left boundary moves to `last[x] + 1`. The special path ending at v starts at any depth in [left, D], giving length `dist_D - dist_left` and node count `D - left + 1`. I update the best (max length, then min node count) using this. On return from v, I restore the boundary and last-occurrence state.

Since the tree is large (up to 5·10^4 nodes), I use an iterative DFS to avoid recursion limits.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-1232", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def longestSpecialPath(self, edges: List[List[int]], nums: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        n = len(nums)\n        adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n        for u, v, w in edges:\n            adj[u].append((v, w))\n            adj[v].append((u, w))\n\n        # path arrays indexed by depth\n        dist = [0] * (n + 1)      # cumulative distance from root at depth d\n        path_val = [0] * (n + 1)  # value at depth d\n        last = {}                  # value -> most recent depth on current path\n        best_len = 0\n        best_nodes = 1\n\n        # iterative DFS: stack entries (node, parent, depth, dist, left, state)\n        # state 0 = enter, 1 = exit (restore)\n        # left boundary stored per node for restore\n        restore_stack = []\n        stack = [(0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, -1)]\n        # tuple: (node, parent, depth, cumdist, state, saved_left, saved_last_prev, val)\n        stack = [(0, -1, 0, 0, 0)]\n        # simpler explicit approach\n        left_of = [0] * n\n        prev_last_of = [None] * n  # (value, previous depth or None)\n\n        def enter(v, p, D, cd, left):\n            x = nums[v]\n            prev = last.get(x)\n            new_left = left\n            if prev is not None and prev >= left:\n                new_left = prev + 1\n            prev_last_of[v] = (x, prev)\n            last[x] = D\n            dist[D] = cd\n            path_val[D] = x\n            left_of[v] = left\n            return new_left\n\n        # iterative with explicit enter/exit\n        work = [(0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 'enter')]\n        cur_left = {}\n        while work:\n            v, p, D, cd, left, action = work.pop()\n            if action == 'enter':\n                new_left = enter(v, p, D, cd, left)\n                # evaluate best special path ending at v\n                length = cd - dist[new_left]\n                nodes = D - new_left + 1\n                if length > best_len or (length == best_len and nodes < best_nodes):\n                    best_len = length\n                    best_nodes = nodes\n                work.append((v, p, D, cd, left, 'exit'))\n                for u, w in adj[v]:\n                    if u != p:\n                        work.append((u, v, D + 1, cd + w, new_left, 'enter'))\n            else:\n                x, prev = prev_last_of[v]\n                if prev is None:\n                    del last[x]\n                else:\n                    last[x] = prev\n\n        if best_len == 0:\n            return [0, 1]\n        return [best_len, best_nodes]\n"}

