class Solution:
    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
I'll use a DP over (index, parity of subsequence length, alternating sum, product) where the product is capped at limit (products above limit are discarded, since nums contains zeros and ones, we must handle the case where a zero makes the product 0 — products are bounded by limit, so we track the set of reachable products ≤ limit).

State: after processing some prefix, for each parity p (number of chosen elements so far mod 2) and alternating sum s, store the set of achievable products ≤ limit. Adding element x at position with parity p: if p is even (this element is at an even index in the subsequence, so it's added), the sum increases by x; if odd, it's subtracted. The new product is prod*x; if it exceeds limit, it's discarded — except that a zero element makes the product 0 regardless of the prior product, so I must also allow products that would exceed limit before multiplying by zero. To handle this, I track whether a zero has been included as a separate flag: once a zero is in the subsequence, the product is 0 and stays 0.

So the DP tracks, for each (parity, sum), the set of capped products plus a boolean "contains zero" which forces product 0. The answer is the maximum product ≤ limit over final states with sum k and nonempty subsequence.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-2347", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:\n        OFFSET = 150 * 12 + 1\n        # state: (parity, sum) -> set of achievable products <= limit (nonempty subsequences)\n        # parity 0 = next element goes at even position (added), parity 1 = next is subtracted\n        dp = {}\n        # zero-containing states are handled as product 0 entries in the set\n        best = -1\n        for x in nums:\n            new = {}\n            # start new subsequence with x (position 0, added)\n            options = [(0, x, x)]  # (parity_before, new_sum_delta, product)\n            cand = []\n            # from empty: start\n            cand.append(((1, x), x))\n            for (par, s), prods in dp.items():\n                if par == 0:\n                    ns = s + x\n                else:\n                    ns = s - x\n                npar = 1 - par\n                for p in prods:\n                    np_ = p * x\n                    if np_ <= limit:\n                        cand.append(((npar, ns), np_))\n            for key, p in cand:\n                new.setdefault(key, set()).add(p)\n            # merge into dp\n            for key, ps in new.items():\n                dp.setdefault(key, set()).update(ps)\n            # also keep the single-element start options\n            dp.setdefault((1, x), set()).add(x) if x <= limit else None\n        for (par, s), prods in dp.items():\n            if s == k:\n                for p in prods:\n                    if p <= limit and p > best:\n                        best = p\n        return best\n"}

